From: EasyStick maintainers <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] sdio: read and write slave registers one byte at a time

Restores the register-access behaviour of the last known-good ESP32-P4 build
(Linux 6.18.35 #29, 2026-08-10 10:48:40 UTC), which reached wlan0.  That build
carried an uncommitted local edit to this file; it was destroyed by a forced
submodule checkout and no upstream commit contains it.  It is reconstructed
here from the retained module binary: in #29, esp_read_reg relocated against
sdio_readb and had no sdio_memcpy_fromio call site at all, while esp_read_block
kept both.

Upstream esp_read_reg/esp_write_reg dispatch on size, so every 4-byte register
access became a single CMD53 IO_RW_EXTENDED transfer.  On this P4/C6 port that
returns zeros, so TOKEN_RDATA, INT_ST and PACKET_LEN all read 0 -- which
presents as "Tx Pos = 0", no boot frame and no wlan0, while the slave still
enumerates normally.  Registers are therefore accessed byte-wise over CMD52.

The CMD53 data path is deliberately untouched: esp_read_block and
esp_write_block keep their size dispatch, so bulk RX/TX still uses
sdio_memcpy_fromio/toio.

What this does not address: why the C6 register window rejects a CMD53
multi-byte read on this host.  The DW-MMC controller, the 0x3FF address mask
and the slave's own window decoding are all still candidates; this change
avoids the transfer rather than explaining it.

---
 sdio/esp_sdio_api.c | 58 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 50 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sdio/esp_sdio_api.c b/sdio/esp_sdio_api.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/sdio/esp_sdio_api.c
+++ b/sdio/esp_sdio_api.c
@@ -107,14 +107,39 @@
 
 int esp_read_reg(struct esp_sdio_context *context, u32 reg, u8 *data, u16 size, u8 is_lock_needed)
 {
+	struct sdio_func *func = NULL;
+	int ret = 0;
+	u16 i;
+
 	/* Need to apply address mask when reading/writing slave registers */
 	reg &= ESP_ADDRESS_MASK;
 
-	if (size <= 1) {
-		return esp_read_byte(context, reg, data, is_lock_needed);
-	} else {
-		return esp_read_multi_byte(context, reg, data, size, is_lock_needed);
+	if (!context || !context->func || !data) {
+		esp_err("Invalid or incomplete arguments!\n");
+		return -1;
 	}
+
+	func = context->func;
+
+	if (is_lock_needed)
+		sdio_claim_host(func);
+
+	/* The ESP slave register window is served reliably over CMD52 only.
+	 * A CMD53 multi-byte read of this window returns zeros on the
+	 * ESP32-P4 host port, which silently zeroes TOKEN_RDATA, INT_ST and
+	 * PACKET_LEN.  Access registers one byte at a time; the CMD53 data
+	 * path is unaffected and stays in esp_read_block().
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < size; i++) {
+		data[i] = sdio_readb(func, reg + i, &ret);
+		if (ret)
+			break;
+	}
+
+	if (is_lock_needed)
+		sdio_release_host(func);
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
 int esp_read_block(struct esp_sdio_context *context, u32 reg, u8 *data, u16 size, u8 is_lock_needed)
@@ -128,14 +153,39 @@
 
 int esp_write_reg(struct esp_sdio_context *context, u32 reg, u8 *data, u16 size, u8 is_lock_needed)
 {
+	struct sdio_func *func = NULL;
+	int ret = 0;
+	u16 i;
+
 	/* Need to apply address mask when reading/writing slave registers */
 	reg &= ESP_ADDRESS_MASK;
 
-	if (size <= 1) {
-		return esp_write_byte(context, reg, *data, is_lock_needed);
-	} else {
-		return esp_write_multi_byte(context, reg, data, size, is_lock_needed);
+	if (!context || !context->func || !data) {
+		esp_err("Invalid or incomplete arguments!\n");
+		return -1;
 	}
+
+	func = context->func;
+
+	if (is_lock_needed)
+		sdio_claim_host(func);
+
+	/* The ESP slave register window is served reliably over CMD52 only.
+	 * A CMD53 multi-byte read of this window returns zeros on the
+	 * ESP32-P4 host port, which silently zeroes TOKEN_RDATA, INT_ST and
+	 * PACKET_LEN.  Access registers one byte at a time; the CMD53 data
+	 * path is unaffected and stays in esp_write_block().
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < size; i++) {
+		sdio_writeb(func, data[i], reg + i, &ret);
+		if (ret)
+			break;
+	}
+
+	if (is_lock_needed)
+		sdio_release_host(func);
+
+	return ret;
 }
 
 int esp_write_block(struct esp_sdio_context *context, u32 reg, u8 *data, u16 size, u8 is_lock_needed)
